layout: tool.njk
tags: tools
permalink: "/tools/n-gram-analyser/"
crumb: "N-gram analyser"
order: 3
heading: "Search terms n-gram analyser for Google Ads"
standfirst: "Paste or upload your search terms report and see which single words and short phrases are spending money without converting. Tick the worst ones and export them as negative keywords."
title: "Search Terms N-gram Analyser for Google Ads | DigitalFadi"
description: "Free n-gram analyser for Google Ads search terms. Find the words and phrases wasting spend across your report, then export them as negative keywords."
faqs:
  - q: "What is an n-gram analysis in Google Ads?"
    a: "It splits every search term in your report into single words (1-grams), pairs of words (2-grams) and three-word phrases (3-grams), then adds up the cost, clicks and conversions of every search that contained each one. A word that appears in 60 different searches, each costing a little, can add up to a large amount of waste that is invisible when you read the report line by line."
  - q: "How do I export a search terms report from Google Ads?"
    a: "In Google Ads open Insights and reports, then Search terms. Set a date range of at least 30 days, or 90 if the account is small, add the Conversions and Conv. value columns if they are missing, then use the download icon and choose CSV. Upload that file here as it is. The tool finds the header row on its own."
  - q: "What date range should I use for an n-gram analysis?"
    a: "Long enough that each phrase has a fair number of clicks. For most accounts that means 60 to 90 days. A short range makes ordinary phrases look like waste simply because they have not had time to convert, so judge a phrase on its clicks and cost, not on a single week."
  - q: "Should I add every zero-conversion n-gram as a negative?"
    a: "No. Check the searches behind each phrase first, which you can do by clicking it in the table. A word like cost might appear in both research searches and buyer searches. Add a negative only when the searches behind it are clearly the wrong intent, and be careful with single words, because a negative blocks every search containing it."
  - q: "Is my search terms data uploaded anywhere?"
    a: "No. The file is read and analysed inside your browser and is never uploaded or sent to any server, including ours. It is not saved either, so the data is gone when you close or refresh the page."
  - q: "Which file formats does the analyser accept?"
    a: "CSV and tab-separated files exported from Google Ads, including the Excel CSV export in UTF-16, plus plain text pasted straight from a spreadsheet. If there are no metric columns it treats each line as one search term and counts how often each phrase appears."
